From 0084d8efac63e61213cdc82dbbc13fb18c76805d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <claude@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 11:10:11 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] logging-switch-error-exception-logs-to-plain-text

Switches the error/exception log files from encrypted to plain text, per explicit confirmation
(the encryption was a deliberate security choice - AES via PBKDF2-derived key, fail-fast at
startup if the secret was missing, same treatment as the JWT signing key - so I flagged the
tradeoff and asked before removing it rather than assuming).

- ApplicationLayer/Options/LogEncryptionOptions.cs replaced by LogFileOptions.cs: Password/Salt
  dropped (nothing to encrypt with any more), file names default to .log instead of .log.enc.
  No appsettings.json section existed for the old options class (Password/Salt were user-secrets
  /environment only, never committed) - LogFile is a new, optional section for the surviving
  Directory/file-name settings, matching this codebase's pattern of every Options class having a
  visible appsettings entry even at its defaults (CardFile, PrintImages).
- InfrastructureLayer/Logging/EncryptedFileSink.cs and LogEncryptor.cs deleted - the last thing
  in this codebase that used either. Program.cs now writes both log files via Serilog's own
  WriteTo.File(formatter, path), same CompactJsonFormatter as before, same Filter.ByIncludingOnly
  split (exceptions file / warning-and-above-without-exception file) - only the encryption step
  is gone, not the structure.
- Program.cs: removed the startup fail-fast check for the encryption password/salt (nothing left
  that needs one) and the now-dead 'using InfrastructureLayer.Logging;'.
- Fixed two doc-comment cross-references in IBatchFileDecryptor.cs and BatchFileCipher.cs that
  pointed at LogEncryptor's ciphertext-layout convention as a naming precedent - both rewritten
  to describe the [nonce][tag][ciphertext] layout directly rather than citing a class that no
  longer exists. Fixed two more in PrintImageOptions.cs and LocalDiskPrintImageStorage.cs that
  cited LogEncryptionOptions.Directory as the precedent for resolving a path relative to
  ContentRootPath - renamed to LogFileOptions.Directory.

Verified: swept the whole codebase for any remaining reference to LogEncryptionOptions,
EncryptedFileSink, LogEncryptor, or the InfrastructureLayer.Logging namespace after the
deletions - none outside LogFileOptions.cs's own doc comment, which mentions the old name only
as historical context. Brace/paren balance and JSON validity checked on every touched file.
